#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""The one entry point for a workflow file - and the reason a stage is not a program.

    easyvm macos/15/steps/ssh.yaml --boot-disk out/macos15-desktop.qcow2 --out out/macos15-ssh.qcow2

WHY THIS EXISTS

Every stage of this repository used to be a Python program with its own `main()`, its own argument
list, and its own copy of "copy the disk, start qemu, run the walkthrough, stop the machine,
compress the result". There are twenty-four such programs and about ten thousand lines between
them, and the part that differs between two stages is almost never the part that was written twice:

    macos/15/ssh.py     852 lines   OpenCore bootstrapping + a YAML walkthrough + a recording
    windows/11/ssh.py   827 lines   the same shape again

A workflow file already says what a stage DOES - atomic steps, one act each. What it could not say
until recently was that it also OWNS A MACHINE. `boot:` and `record:` are steps now, so it can, and
this program is what is left to run: read the file, give it a disk, get out of the way.

WHAT STILL LIVES IN PYTHON, STATED PLAINLY

Some of a stage is not a step and cannot become one:

    building the qemu command line     lib/vm.py, per family - OpenCore, OSK, virtio-serial, smc
    preparing the disk                 an overlay, a copy, or a move - lib/working_copy.py
    publishing                         compress, digest, push - different programmes

Those are facts about MACHINES, not actions on a SCREEN, and `click:` cannot express them. They
belong in `lib/` and this program calls them. That migration is happening one stage at a time: a
repository that cannot run is a repository that cannot be verified, and ten thousand lines is not a
change anybody can check in one go.

HOW THE RULE IS ENFORCED

`easyvm` is the only entry point that is supported. A stage's own `main()` still works while it is
being migrated - the workflow files call it - but it says so on stderr, and
`EASYVM_STRICT=1` turns that into a refusal:

    $ EASYVM_STRICT=1 python3 macos/15/ssh.py --disk x
    === THIS STAGE IS NOT AN ENTRY POINT ===
      run it through its workflow file instead:
        easyvm macos/15/steps/ssh.yaml --boot-disk x
      EASYVM_STRICT is set, so this is a refusal rather than a warning.

USAGE

    easyvm WORKFLOW [--boot-disk PATH] [--boot-iso PATH] [--out PATH] [--work DIR]
                    [--boot-mem MB] [--boot-cpus N] [--boot-timeout S]
                    [--ssh-port N] [--user NAME] [--password PW] [--list] [--check]

`--boot-disk` is required by a file that declares `boot:`; a file that drives an ALREADY RUNNING
guest takes `--qmp SOCKET` instead. That is the rule lib/steps.py documents, and this program does
not add a second one.

`--boot-iso` IS THE OTHER RUN FACT A FILE CANNOT HOLD. It is the installer medium, and only some
families read it: `os: android` uses its presence to choose between the INSTALLER shape (this ISO
as a cdrom, `-kernel`/`-initrd` out of the ISO's own directory) and the INSTALLED-DISK shape (one
drive, no kernel, the firmware reads the ESP's GRUB). A file that installs names it; the same file
with it omitted starts the system it produced, which is why the two android stages are two files
and not one with a flag in it.

IT TAKES THE DIRECTORY `fetch_iso.py --out` WROTE, NOT ONLY THE FILE. The ISO and the three
members extracted out of it are one set, and naming the set is the statement; one `.iso` in the
directory is what makes it unambiguous.
"""

from __future__ import annotations

import os
import sys

REPO = os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(__file__))
sys.path.insert(0, REPO)
sys.path.insert(0, os.path.join(REPO, "lib"))


def main(argv: list[str]) -> int:
    if not argv:
        print(__doc__, file=sys.stderr)
        return 2
    if argv[0] in ("-h", "--help"):
        print(__doc__)
        return 0

    # `--list` AND `--check` ARE QUESTIONS ABOUT THE FILE and pass straight through - same parser,
    # so a second implementation of "what keys does a step accept" cannot drift from the first.
    import steps

    return steps.main(argv)


if __name__ == "__main__":
    raise SystemExit(main(sys.argv[1:]))
